 "seat": {
  "BEST_WHY": "The lit floor in 3 reads as a cut and worn surface, not a stamp. Slab sizes vary along a single row (the row at y\u2248560\u2013600 has a wide slab at x\u2248420\u2013520 next to narrow ones at x\u2248560\u2013600), and cracks cross joints \u2014 the long crack that starts near the crate at (300,500) runs down through (380,560) to (450,620) across three slabs, which is what a real fracture does. The wall band at y\u2248420\u2013460 is a dark stone face with a horizontal timber run and driven pins at roughly x=300, 390, 460, 590, 680, and the timber sits in front of the stone with its own shadow, so the \"repaired with rope and pins\" fiction is drawn, not implied. The unlit rock mass (upper-left 200\u2013440 \u00d7 80\u2013380, lower-left 60\u2013200 \u00d7 700\u2013900) is crisp at full resolution with fracture lines at two scales \u2014 long faults and small chips \u2014 so darkness reads as darkness over rock rather than as a blur. The figure's light falls off smoothly and the brazier at (595,545) casts its own warm pool that merges with the main one without a seam.",
  "WORST_WHY": "There is no light falloff anywhere \u2014 the frame is one flat exposure edge to edge, so the carried-light premise isn't drawn at all. The grey wall at left (x 0\u201350, y 0\u2013190) and the whole bottom band (y 195\u2013288) are the same stamped brick pattern, every brick the same rectangle at the same value, with no top-surface/face distinction; the bottom band consequently reads as both wall and floor at once (moss blobs are placed on it at (30,215) as if it were floor, but it uses the wall texture). The dirt floor is a single repeating speckle tile with the tile grid faintly visible through it. The water at right (x 250\u2013340) is a flat green fill with soft round blobs that meets the dirt on a hard straight vertical line with no bank, edge or depth. The \"bones\" at (215\u2013235, 115\u2013125) are a handful of loose white pixels. Crates, barrels and the figure carry heavy black outlines while the terrain has none. Taken together it reads as a free tileset assembled in an editor.",
  "FLAGGED": "1, 2, 4",
  "RANK": "3 > 2 > 4 > 1",
  "BEST": "3",
  "WORST": "1",
  "SHIP": "NONE"
 },
